  "narration": {
    "title": "Milwaukee's Bats Run Hot as the Week Turns",
    "intro": "This one is about who's moving. The Brewers had two hitters torch the week, a few big names cooled off, and a couple of arms slipped a notch. Here's the week's real motion.",
    "risers_lead": "Garrett Mitchell owned the week for Milwaukee, going 9-for-16 with two homers and eight RBI for a 1.757 OPS, and teammate Brice Turang wasn't far behind at 1.538. Alex Bregman, Nolan Arenado and Kyle Tucker all cleared 1.380, with Tucker driving in nine over seven games.",
    "pfallers_lead": "Nobody among the elite arms tumbled hard this week; Dylan Cease slipped a single point of form to 79, which is noise, not news. The board up top held remarkably steady.",
    "hweek_lead": "Same names as the risers own the week's plate: Mitchell and Turang gave Milwaukee a pair of blistering lines, and Arenado's three homers in six games carried Arizona's week.",
    "fallers_lead": "Yandy D\u00edaz had the cold week that counts, 2-for-26 across six games and down 29 points of season OPS, while Luis Garc\u00eda Jr. went 4-for-20 and dipped 20 points. Neither is a collapse, just a quiet stretch.",
    "colors_lead": "No hitter or arm crossed a tier line this week, which is its own kind of story this late: the color map has largely set. Alvarez still glows at a 1.027 OPS and Misiorowski at a 95 form, right where they were."
  }
